    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-4828804">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-4828804">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4828804)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4828804">
																		Re: The Ophanim
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eads" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1697517); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1697517&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606616188" alt="Eads" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1697517)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eads" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1697517); return false;" >Eads</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606420313 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Nov 2020 19:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4828804">
									

<p>In general: The concept kept me going, and the pacing is okay. The dialogue is fine, but the description gets in the way during the logs. There is a large amount of conflicting or unexplained information that makes following the article difficult. That's mostly outside of the logs that you're worried about. The logs themselves have a lot of problems with tone.</p>
<p>Here are my thoughts and recommendations, roughly in order:</p>
<p>Object classification: This is the first and will probably be the last time I have ever recommend this, but given that this thing is causing an ongoing, unstoppable and exponentially increasing worldwide rapture&#8230; We're probably beyond Keter. I don't know what the fancy apocalypse object class du jour is these days, but I would recommend dialing the sheer scope of your SCP back if you don't want to deal with the scrutiny that comes with a zany object class.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;SCProcedures</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;SCProcedures</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Physical containment of SCP-XXXX is impossible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is not worth including.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Several Mark II Self-Sustaining Cumulous Steam Generators (SSCSG) are to orbit around SCP-XXXX. Meteorological data is to be fed to each SSCSG in order to maintain a thick cloud around SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Mostly good, &quot;thick cloud&quot; doesn't seem clinical enough in tone.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Outside containment consultants, as well as several members of GOI-### (&quot;The Horizon Initiative&quot;) have been contracted to isolate the region of the Noosphere containing concepts of and relating to SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's a small o in GoI.</p>
<p>I took a brief look around the site and apparently it's established enough that a handful of articles use it freely with no context, but I spent a good chunk of this article wondering if you were ever going to explain what the Noosphere was. After Googling it, I spent the rest of the time wondering why we were glossing over the metaphysical concept of the collective unconscious being a physical space that an MTF can just stroll on into.</p>
<p>This is probably going to tie into most other places where I was confused. If there's a master definition of this somewhere, it should be linked and embedded since it's crucial to following the plot of your article.</p>
<p>Additionally, the final log makes it seem like XXXX got bodied all the way out of the Noosphere. The Horizon Initiative contracting already occurred, and doesn't seem like it has any bearing on the current containment procedures.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation agents embedded in religious organizations are to pursuade as many followers into worship of SCP-XXXX as possible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Persuade. Also, why?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following phrase is to be repeated by those participating in the containment of SCP-XXXX:</p>
<p>&gt; Be not afraid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Repeated how? In what way? At specific times? Participating in the containment under what conditions? Also, again, <em>why?</em></p>
<p>The article does not make it clear, and it also makes the later shift of Raynes' name to the phrase confusing. To be honest, it doesn't serve much purpose in either instance&#8212;it's just here because you know those wacky Abrahamic Angels and their catchphrase, they just feel bad about being so <em>goshdarn spooky.</em></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Description</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Description</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Whether or not the flame is responsible for SCP-XXXX's ability to remain airborne is unclear.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's no particular reason to think this, and this line doesn't add anything.</p>
<p>Also: Do you need a reason to justify remaining airborne in the <em>metaphysical concept of the collective unconsciousness?</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>At this time, there is no method of preventing a Pencerahan Event, or ceasing one in progress.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure there is: the description says it only works on people who are aware of SCP-XXXX. You can prevent it by making sure they don't know about it. Actually, given that, shouldn't the instructions in the SCProcedures be to convince as many people as possible to <em>stop</em> worshiping SCP-XXXX? Or to try and axe the Abrahamic faiths entirely?</p>
<p>Actually, this thing isn't <em>God</em>, it's God's hype man for the rapture. I haven't been to church in quite a while, but you don't really worship angels, right?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A pile of salt<br />
A pile of ash<br />
Brimstone</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I like where this ends up going. I don't think it needs to be rendered in bullet points, however. That just makes it distracting. &quot;Transform&quot; and &quot;piles of&quot; also aren't terribly clinical.</p>
<p>The description seems inconsistent on whether they just turn into salt or burn alive. I would recommend uniformly burning alive, and something to the effect of &quot;leaving behind trace elements of either carbon, sodium or sulfur.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(In footnote 3) All Foundation staff that participated in this experiment expressed disgust and concern for the D-Class once it had concluded. Several resigned.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Your Foundation personnel are <em>babies.</em> Especially given that people are apparently getting unwillingly raptured worldwide and the nature of their entire job and organization is to prevent and contain paranormal events. Burning up some convicts for the greater good hardly seems like it should be the barf-inducing final straw.</p>
<p>The actual table looks fine (I would still recommend the change to &quot;carbon, sodium or sulfur&quot; so that it's not so in-your-face that they're going straight to the Actual Abrahamic Hell That Exists). You do use &quot;turns&quot; instead of &quot;turned&quot; a handful of times.</p>
<p>Since you're leaning into religious imagery here: All the people with really severe crimes going to hell is straightforward. Adding a note in there about someone with a prior history of murder who has displayed conversion to a life of faith turning into salt, or someone who committed minor crimes but never repented turning into brimstone would go a long way towards giving this article more depth.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Pencerahan Events</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The language on which thing causes what was already a bit confusing, but this one drives it home. At different points in the article, we have these descriptions:</p>
<p>1. When people directly view SCP-XXXX, they confess their sins and then explode.<br />
2. When people are told SCP-XXXX exists, but don't directly view it, they cannot tell a lie, develop peace and good will about mankind, man, and then turn into salt, uniformly (apparently learning about it but not seeing it means you go up instead of down).<br />
3. When people are &quot;aware&quot; of it, but don't know it exists, they have an emotional breakdown, strip, and then pelvic-thrust into the sky. This is different from being told it exists, for reasons that are unclear.</p>
<p>You need to clarify these concepts and marry them together. I personally don't think the concept of a Pencerahan Event as described actually adds anything here. You seem attached to the term, so you could consider using it for the ongoing worldwide rapture occurring in the article, instead of each individual that gets raptured. I think getting raptured or exploding into heaven/hell/purgatory substance should all just be considered and logged as potential effects of exposure.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;The&nbsp;Logs</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;The&nbsp;Logs</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>You need more context for these. The fact that people are getting raptured left and right feels like an End-of-the-World Scenario, but it's glossed over. A vague percentage, the difficulty in containment, the ticking clock that billions will burn alive and throw the world into chaos&#8212;drive that home. Make us believe the Foundation would be willing to break the wheels of the chariot of God to save humanity. Ignoring Secure and Contain in order to Protect is a <em>big</em> step you cannot skip over if the Foundation has decided to merc an anomaly.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>MTF Zeta-9 (Mole Rats)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They do specialize in places that don't follow normal physics and space/time, and I'm assuming the improbably physical nature of the Noosphere has some of that going on. But it never actually comes up during the article, so it feels like you just included them because they're the neat ones people like.</p>
<p>(I am people. God bless our scuttling bois, the Mole Rats are my favorites. &lt;3)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&lt;Begin Log&gt;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The actual dialogue in this section is fine.</p>
<p>The descriptions at times stray from clinical, but that is tricky to fix while still describing a Spooky Abrahamic Angel. Most of my problems aren't in this one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Someone in the background expresses disapproval, but what is said is unheard.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Unintelligible&quot; would be a better word choice, but things like this are always a little awkward to include in logs.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foxtrot steps into the fire and is consumed by it. She does not scream.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This one's probably the least clinical line in this log. You could just cut it down to &quot;Foxtrot steps into the fire.&quot; Remember, this is for professional purposes&#8212;if the Foundation is writing it, it's not going to be anything but bare-bones descriptive.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Containment Consultant Meeting</p>
<p>In attendance: O5-7, Researcher Raynes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Also, this Woo person.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>O5-7: Now, before we begin I feel that it is imperative that we-</p>
<p>Raynes: Xir, with all due respect</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like cutting off an O5 at a professional meeting with another GoI should get you fired. Into the sun.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>O5-7: Take some time off, doctor. I need you level headed for what's to come next.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why does it have to be Raynes? After that outburst, it feels like they should have picked someone else. An event this large cannot have only Raynes working on it as a researcher. There is no compelling reason presented to use someone who is emotionally unstable and bearing the guilt of multiple deaths while the fate of humanity is at stake.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>you mind giving us some elucidation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a really strange turn of phrase.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>U-90 Alpha fires at it to no avail. U-90 Beta and Raynes attempt to flee but are unable to get passed the Horizon Initative.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;No avail&quot; doesn't seem clinical. You meant &quot;past.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Immediately following tge Initiative's egress from the Noosphere, an unknown entity manifested below SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, the Noosphere is not on Earth, even if it's a physical place. Woo even refers to it as the theoretical realm. How do they know what happened in the Noosphere after the Initiative left?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The unknown entity was roughly humanoid, and had innumerable wings that allowed it to maintain self-sustained flight. The entity had several thousand faces as well, and each of it's wings were lined with eyes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Getting too flowery. Not clinical enough.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The unknown entity elevated upward, knocking SCP-XXXX into the stratosphere.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Wait, so you can punch something out of the collective unconscious?</p>
<p>&#8230;That's honestly badass, but it probably bears more description that Raynes <em>turned into a megazord and punched the Chariot of God into another dimension.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite the overwhelming evidence supporting the existence of 'Researcher Raynes', there are no records in file of any Foundation personnel bearing that moniker.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are there no records or is there overwhelming evidence? Where's the discrepancy? This doesn't feel like it ties into anything at all.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following Incident T-2:1-10, the name 'Raynes' exhibits a mild anti-memetic effect, rendering anyone unable to vocalize it. When subjects attempt to speak the word 'Raynes', they instead speak the phrase 'Be not afraid.'.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm pretty sure that's a memetic effect, not an anti-memetic effect. Also, the logistics of not being able to say the name &quot;Raynes&quot; is non-trivial. (For one, you'd never be able to sing Africa again.) Consider making that effect apply to the full name only.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Overall&nbsp;Suggestions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Overall&nbsp;Suggestions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Procedures: These are largely fine, but could use a little tidying up for tone and clarity. The &quot;Be Not Afraid&quot; thing is confusing and doesn't add much. The bit about worshiping SCP-XXXX makes no sense.</p>
<p>Description: You need to pin down the effects of this thing and make them feel consistent. The ideas are neat, with some tweaking so they're a little less on the nose. As it is, they're contradictory and that needs to be resolved.</p>
<p>As sold as I am on the concept of punching God's Secretary of Rapture into another dimension, I don't think the Noosphere actually adds anything to the article. I haven't been keeping close tabs on the wiki over the past ~2 years as it's continued to blow up, so it's possible I am the last person here to learn about the Noosphere. For my money at least, all it does is make the article vastly more confusing.</p>
<p>It's a really big concept to introduce if you're not going to use it, and to be honest, you don't. They go into the Noosphere, yes&#8212;they could just as easily go to whatever physical location the Ophanim parked itself on, it doesn't really make a difference to the story structure. It has a cognitohazardous effect that reaches people through the Noosphere, yes. You could also just have that be a thing that it does from wherever it's sitting on Earth. Since it seems like the Mole Rats go in there and die (as my poor scuttly bois are wont to do), with their physical bodies, there's no unorthodox ramifications to dying in the Noosphere. There's no strange topography or space/time shenanigans due to its presence in the story.</p>
<p>I recommend hacking it out entirely. If you're not willing to do so, I would recommend making it more clear what the Noosphere is, and why it's crucial to the article and nature of the anomaly.</p>
<p>The Logs: Your dialogue is fine (give or take that I think Raynes shouldn't be able to mouth off to an O5). The descriptions need to be a bit more clinical, and the last one needs to be <em>much</em> more clinical if you want it to be a Foundation document.</p>
<p>This is a suggestion, but given the tone in the last Incident Log, and that it concerns events that it seems like only the Horizon Initiative could have witnessed. Instead of being presented as official Foundation documentation, it could be a personal document submitted by the Initiative (or Woo personally) as a description for the Foundation files.</p>
<p>If you want to keep the idea that the Foundation has somehow forgotten he exists, you can then follow that up with the gut-punch: <em>Despite the previous correspondence from Woo, the Foundation has no personnel records for a Researcher of that name. Video logs, likewise provided by the Horizon Initiative, have been recorded on equipment that matches Foundation standard body cameras. However, the footage frequently cuts out and is replaced with a black screen, with white text that reads &quot;BE NOT AFRAID.&quot;</em></p>
<p>That said, the Foundation forgetting Raynes exists but not SCP-XXXX doesn't make a whole lot of sense for that ending. There's nothing that alludes to the COB burning him out of the Foundation personnel files, and it doesn't match up to the behavior of SCP-XXXX. If <em>that's</em> the ultimate role of the Noosphere in this story, somehow, it needs to be made clear.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, the idea is neat and you sold me pretty heavily on suplexing an angel into low-space, but it needs cleaning, technically and conceptually. You also need to pin down the timeline of what happens when. The early portions of the article make it sound like the rapture is still happening, the last logs seem to imply it's over and just needs some clouds sprayed on it to keep it contained. With revision, this could be great.</p>

							</div>

								
					
